\section*{A non-Gaussian massive continuum theory from the planar O(3) model}

\subsection{OpenAI's claim}
OpenAI presents a proof in the selected manuscript. The two-dimensional $O(3)$ lattice model is claimed to have a unique interacting massive continuum limit under susceptibility and correlation-length normalization.

\subsection{Outline of the argument}
A prescribed physical block scale first produces limiting Schwinger fields with uniform finite-volume and moment estimates. Renormalization comparisons remove subsequence dependence and convert the block prescription to canonical normalization; reflection positivity and clustering reconstruct a gapped theory with a nonzero connected four-point function.

\subsection{Scope relative to this problem}
The two-dimensional $O(3)$ lattice model is claimed to have a unique interacting massive continuum limit under susceptibility and correlation-length normalization. The question retains these same parameters and conventions. Within these assumptions, this is a claimed affirmative answer to the stated question.

\subsection{Formal verification}
For family 215, the selected theorem gives exponential finite-free-graph $O(n)$ spin decay for $n\geq3$ and bounded ferromagnetic edge strengths. Plane limits, full $O(4)$ spectral gaps, and continuum $O(3)$ results are beyond that entry. No Comparator is assigned to the selected manuscript here; the family page selects other papers or supporting statements. This site has not executed the Lean code. The scope descriptions do not certify the remaining manuscript arguments.

\subsection{Status caveat}
OpenAI attributes these manuscripts to work by an internal model. Its release cautions that arguments without formalization can contain mistakes. This catalogue has not independently checked the proofs; the labels report the mathematical claims attributed to OpenAI.

